# Revision history for PAGI-Tools

0.002003 - UNRELEASED
  - BREAKING: Replaced the mutable, request-bound PAGI::Response builder with
    complete concrete Response values and exported factories: Response::Text,
    ::HTML, ::JSON, ::Problem, ::Redirect, ::Empty, ::File, and ::Stream.
    Response is invoked only through `to_app` or the generic `invoke_app`
    helper; the public `respond` method, Request's response bridge, and the
    generic text/html/json/send/send_raw/
    redirect/empty/send_file/stream/writer finishers are removed. Buffered
    response bodies are byte strings; Text and HTML perform explicit charset
    encoding, while File and Stream retain non-buffered delivery ownership.
    Stream Writer writes are awaitable backpressure points under PAGI's send-
    settlement contract, and disconnect settles pending writes without
    counting discarded bytes. File validates the filesystem at request-time
    preflight, applies logical windows before Range policy, and opts out of
    `body-events-v1` so PAGI servers may preserve file events.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Pages class methods, configured-instance methods, and
    opt-in exports are source-free factories for deferred HTTP applications.
    Negotiation uses the invocation scope. Route and Mount retain their
    distinct exact-leaf/method/Allow versus prefix/path-rewrite/subtree
    ownership. App::File's application-root
    constructor is `from_app_path`, while PAGI::Utils::app_path remains a path
    utility. WebSocket `deny` and SSE `decline` now accept a finite Response;
    accepted WebSocket/SSE sessions remain live protocol loops, not HTTP
    Response applications.
  - BREAKING: Route CODE endpoints receive one Request, WebSocket, or SSE;
    Route application objects own the native PAGI triplet. Mount, Compose, and
    Router defaults retain the native CODE contract. HTTP handler results are
    native CODE or instantiated `to_app` objects. Native Route CODE uses
    `as_app`; GET plus automatic HEAD is the default, while scalar
    `methods => '*'` is unrestricted. Object `allowed_methods` is snapshotted
    once at Route construction, so routed Endpoint::HTTP objects contribute
    verbs and OPTIONS while Router owns unsupported-method 405/Allow outcomes.
    Response and Pages applications retain their exact configured objects;
    Request headers and Lifespan request scopes likewise retain their exact
    request-owned identities rather than promising defensive copies.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Middleware::Lint no longer keeps its own copy of
    send-sequencing state; it delegates event-order legality (duplicate
    http.response.start, body before start, sends after completion,
    illegal trailers, and so on) to the shared PAGI::SendValidation core
    also used by PAGI::Test::Client. Lint's previous hand-written warnings
    for these cases ("http.response.start sent multiple times",
    "http.response.body sent after response finished (more=0)") are gone;
    the same violations are now caught with a core-derived, app-contextual
    message instead (e.g. "app sent 'http.response.body' out of order
    (cannot send http.response.body: response already complete) -- check
    the order your app calls $send in for this response"). In strict mode,
    a shared-core violation now rejects the event outright (never
    forwarded, the call fails) instead of warning and forwarding it as
    before. Lint's own remaining checks are the complementary ones a
    server can't give: a connection-specific header (connection,
    transfer-encoding) set by the app on http.response.start warns with
    app context before forwarding (always forwarded -- the server
    enforces the strip); trailers declared via trailers => 1 but never
    sent by app return get a friendly diagnostic distinct from the core's
    bare "incomplete" error; and overlapping in-flight sends (a second
    $send issued before the previous one was awaited) warn in both strict
    and non-strict mode, since PAGI leaves overlap unspecified rather than
    illegal. Also fixed a stale POD claim that http.response.body's more
    key was validated for presence -- it never was; the real more-key rule
    (absent/false/file/fh marks a chunk terminal, and a further body event
    after that is illegal) is now genuinely enforced, via the shared core.
  - BREAKING: Removed PAGI::Middleware::WebSocket::RateLimit: it impersonated
    server lifecycle events it could not keep faithful (ghost reason-less
    repeated disconnects, fire-and-forget close). Rate limiting is app
    policy; a receive-loop recipe is coming in a later Cookbook pass.
  - BREAKING: Removed PAGI::App::SSE::Pubsub, PAGI::App::WebSocket::Broadcast,
    and PAGI::App::WebSocket::Chat: they taught a send pattern the PAGI spec
    now rules out (holding and calling another scope's send). Real fan-out
    belongs to the coming PAGI-Channels distribution (own-scope
    receive-multiplexed messaging).
  - PAGI::App::WebSocket::Echo's on_disconnect callback now receives
    ($scope, $code, $reason) instead of ($scope, $code), so handlers can see
    why the peer disconnected.
  - HEAD requests to routes that declare trailers (trailers => 1) no longer
    abort the connection: both HEAD wire boundaries (the automatic
    Compose/routing boundary and PAGI::Middleware::Head) now strip the
    trailers declaration from the forwarded response start when they
    suppress the trailers themselves, so the response reaches a legal
    terminal state instead of promising trailers that never arrive.
  - PAGI::Middleware::ConditionalGet now suppresses every event the wrapped
    application sends after its synthesized 304 (previously only body
    events), so a trailer-bearing cacheable route no longer fails on
    revalidation hits.
  - PAGI::Middleware::ETag no longer emits a malformed response start with
    an undefined status when the wrapped application never started a
    response; it now emits nothing and lets the server's no-response 500
    backstop report the real problem.
  - PAGI::Middleware::GZip hardcoded `status => 200`
    on every response it compressed -- a 404, a 500, a redirect, anything --
    any response through GZip with a gzip-negotiating client went out as a
    silent 200. It now captures and replays the wrapped application's real
    status at all three of its response-start emission sites.
  - PAGI::Middleware::GZip mirrors ETag's no-start guard: it no longer
    emits a response start with an undefined status when the wrapped
    application never started a response (emits nothing instead, same
    backstop as ETag), and defaults headers to an empty arrayref rather
    than undef when a start event omits them.
  - PAGI::Middleware::ETag, ::GZip, and ::ContentLength no longer destroy or
    mis-frame opaque (file/fh) response bodies. A body event carrying a
    `file` or `fh` key has no `body` string to buffer -- previously ETag
    hashed the empty string, GZip silently dropped the file entirely, and
    ContentLength declared `content-length: 0` beside a real file body. All
    three now recognize such events via the new shared
    `PAGI::Middleware::body_event_is_opaque` predicate and disengage,
    forwarding the file/fh event through untouched instead.
  - PAGI::Middleware::ContentLength no longer skips
    Content-Length synthesis just because the wrapped application set its
    own `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` header on a single-shot (non-streamed)
    response. An app cannot actually produce chunked framing itself -- that
    is a server/transport concern, and the server strips any app-supplied
    `Transfer-Encoding` header before the wire -- so honoring that header
    only left such a response with neither a Content-Length nor real
    chunked framing. A single-shot body now gets an accurate
    Content-Length regardless; use `auto_chunked` or a streamed (`more =>
    1`) body to opt out of synthesis.
  - PAGI::SSE's `every()` could cancel the live protocol receive.
    Its background disconnect monitor directly awaits `$receive->()`;
    `every()` used to race that same future against a sleep timer via
    `Future->wait_any`, which cancels whichever subfuture loses -- so on
    nearly every tick (sleep wins almost always, since the monitor's
    future only resolves on a real disconnect), the in-flight
    `$receive->()` call was cancelled. `_watch_for_disconnect` now returns
    a decoupled signal future that it resolves manually once the monitor
    stops, instead of returning the receive-suspended future itself;
    racing, losing, or cancelling that signal no longer touches the live
    receive.
  - PAGI::Test::Client's H1-flavored response pass now supplies the
    Connection: upgrade companion when an application response carries an
    Upgrade header, matching the real server's RFC 9110 obligation (added
    to PAGI::Server in 0.002008); previously the mock omitted it, so the
    426 Upgrade Required pattern looked different under test than live.
  - PAGI::Middleware::Head is now a thin delegate to the shared HEAD wire
    boundary implementation (one implementation, two entry points); using
    it under Compose remains harmless but redundant.
  - BREAKING: Removed the entire `PAGI` Context class family, without a
    compatibility alias or replacement hook. Normal HTTP, WebSocket, and SSE
    callbacks receive `PAGI::Request`, `PAGI::WebSocket`, and `PAGI::SSE`
    directly; HTTP callbacks return immediate or Future-backed application
    values, commonly concrete Responses from the class/factory family. URL
    generation, stash, session, state, CSRF, and transport access use their
    explicitly imported owning helpers. ErrorHandler custom renderers receive
    `($request, $error)` and must return a Response; an explicit renderer
    status wins over the derived exception/configured status. Removed
    along with the class family are Endpoint factory overrides, protocol
    assertions and type maps, raw channel accessors, the generic event
    dispatcher, and the cached HTTP Response accumulator plus guarded send.
    ErrorHandler's fallback-status mechanism remains. Native PAGI applications
    and all middleware retain
    `($scope, $receive, $send)`.
    Extension protocols provide their own object. Endpoint disconnect hooks are
    synchronous. `PAGI::Request->response` is removed.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::SSE no longer sends `sse.start` after
    `on_connect` has already declined the request -- previously `handle()`
    unconditionally awaited `run()` regardless of what `on_connect` did, so
    a concrete-Response decline left a stray `sse.start` on the wire after
    the response was already complete, a first-send-wins violation the
    server must raise on. `PAGI::SSE::decline($response)` gives apps a finite
    HTTP denial API (parity with `PAGI::WebSocket::deny($response)`): it sends
    an eligible Response, marks the connection closed, drops any keepalive
    requested before `on_connect` ran, and turns `start`/`run`/`send`/
    `send_json`/`send_event`/`send_comment`/`keepalive` into safe no-ops
    afterward. `handle()` also now skips `run()` outright once the
    connection is already closed. Declining from `on_connect` is documented
    as the supported auth-gate pattern.
  - [BUGFIX] Every PAGI::Endpoint::SSE endpoint using `keepalive_interval`
    was broken against any server enforcing the SSE send-sequencing rules
    (both the reference server's `EventValidator` and PAGI::SendValidation
    reject `sse.keepalive` sent before `sse.start`), because `handle()`
    configures the keepalive before `on_connect`/`start` ever runs --
    silently tolerated by older, non-validating servers, but a hard failure
    on every request against a conforming one. PAGI::SSE::keepalive() now
    defers: called before the stream has started, it records the requested
    interval/comment instead of sending, and `start()` arms the recording
    (sends the real event) immediately afterward, where it is legal.
    API-compatible -- existing callers are unaffected, the wire event just
    moves to after `sse.start`. A decline (see above) drops an unarmed
    recording instead of needing to disarm it, since it was never sent.

  - New PAGI::Pages constructs ordinary negotiated Responses and coderef terminal
    endpoints for the fixed PAGI welcome page, registered or strict custom
    4xx/5xx problems, and 301/302/303/307/308 redirects. It supports HTML,
    RFC 9457 problem JSON or ordinary JSON, and text; synchronous subclass
    render hooks; exact-status embedded SVG favicons; validated semantic HTTP
    fields; safe no-store error defaults; and fragment-correct optional query
    preservation.
  - BREAKING: the standalone NotFound and Redirect applications are removed.
    Pages replaces their terminal uses; redirects now default
    `preserve_query` to false. ErrorHandler removes `content_type`, negotiates
    its built-in output through Pages, and uses its existing handler seam for a
    fixed representation. See UPGRADING.md for exact replacements.
  - Generic first-party HTTP error and Location-redirect defaults now delegate
    representation construction to Pages while each component retains its
    trigger, status, protocol facts, and existing custom-response seam. This
    adds required File 405/416 and Static 416 fields, stops JSONBody decoder
    detail leakage, validates authentication realms and redirect codes, and
    fixes query placement before redirect fragments. Loader, URLMap, and
    Throttle now reject their incompatible non-HTTP fallback cases clearly.
  - Shared Accept matching now honors the most-specific effective quality, so
    exact q=0 exclusions are not revived by positive wildcards and equal
    qualities retain server order. Malformed or missing scope types croak
    instead of being treated as HTTP; extension protocols own their validation
    and protocol object.
  - BREAKING: Route matches a complete URL leaf. Mount composes an application
    under a prefix. Router selects and owns routing outcomes. Middleware wraps
    behavior. Compose owns the application root and lifespan. This redesign is
    recorded in unreleased 0.002003 and lands before the next CPAN release with
    no compatibility layer.
  - HTTP Router exhaustion is now a complete outcome. NONE invokes the
    Router's native `http_default` or stock negotiated Pages 404; PARTIAL emits
    the built-in negotiated 405 with one authoritative first-seen `Allow`
    union. The default is HTTP-only and never replaces WebSocket/SSE misses,
    selected exceptions, handler-returned 404s, or PARTIAL. The immutable,
    App, and Endpoint frontends reject removed `not_found` and
    `method_not_allowed` callbacks.
  - PAGI::Compose no longer creates routing Trace/fallback layers. It owns the
    root lifespan and installs an outer idempotent application-root HEAD
    boundary, ErrorHandler, and a response-completion guard outside author
    middleware and the target. A directly compiled Router already installs its
    own HeadBoundary. Router 404/405 responses pass through unchanged. A
    selected native application that sends nothing is guarded as 500, not
    reinterpreted as a routing miss.
  - ErrorHandler adds an optional Response renderer, awaits immediate or
    Future-backed `on_error` reporting, and rethrows the original exception
    after response start without attempting a second response. Its built-in
    Pages-backed responses use byte-correct UTF-8 Content-Length and
    `Cache-Control: no-store`; a supplied handler remains authoritative.
    Ordinary ErrorHandler development mode remains static; only Compose's
    private failsafe resolves the environment per handled request.
  - PAGI::App::Cascade now advances only on explicit non-final response
    statuses in `catch`, streams non-caught responses after start, and rejects
    silent HTTP children. Router 404/405 responses participate like any other
    response. PAGI::App::URLMap remains opaque but Router children are complete
    for their own outcomes. See UPGRADING.md for exact Router, Mount, URLMap,
    Cascade, and ErrorHandler migrations.
  - PAGI::Utils now exports `pagi_env` and the four environment predicates
    through `:env` and `:all`; unset or empty PAGI_ENV safely means production,
    while invalid nonempty values croak. PAGI::App::File now uses
    `is_development` at its existing request-time diagnostic boundary.
  - PAGI::App::File adds the class-only `app_path` constructor, which derives
    a static root from each importing caller's application home. In development
    it prints each valid candidate path to STDOUT. All example applications
    using PAGI::App::File now use this constructor. The Perl 5.40 large
    application relies on its version declaration for strictures and warnings.
  - BREAKING: rooted File, Directory, and Static request paths now share one
    lexical/no-I/O contract. Configured symlinks are trusted administrator
    layout rather than rejected outward targets; hidden files are forbidden by
    default, and the historical `show_hidden` listing-only option is replaced
    by the `allow_hidden` serving/listing policy. Directory missing and method
    results now distinguish 404 from 403 and return 405 with `Allow: GET, HEAD`
    for unsupported methods. See UPGRADING.md for the exact behavior table and
    deployment threat model.
  - XSendfile hash mappings now match filesystem components, choose the most
    specific normalized source prefix deterministically, and leave unmatched
    original PAGI response/file events unintercepted. The chat examples now
    delegate non-API HTTP requests to one caller-relative PAGI::App::File;
    Cookbook and Tutorial custom download recipes validate lexical paths,
    authorization, and request-derived response headers separately.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::App::Router and PAGI::Endpoint::Router are now mutable and
    method-oriented frontends over the immutable PAGI::Routing engine. The old
    App matcher, native-by-default route handlers, route-first/mount-fallback
    ordering, dotted names, package-string loading, Endpoint private state and
    context override, and response-valued middleware chain are removed without
    a compatibility mode. See UPGRADING.md for exercised before-and-after
    migrations to direct protocol handlers, explicit application Routes, local names and slash
    addresses, explicit constructed child objects, retained snapshots, and
    universal native middleware. `group`, positional Mount targets, and
    `router =>` are removed. Functional `mount(..., routes => [...])` takes a
    structural node array; mutable `mount(..., routes => sub { ... })` builds a
    real child Router, and Endpoint `app_as` exposes local native methods.
  - PAGI::Routing inline paths now accept zero-argument constraint providers
    such as `{id:&Int}`. Providers use exact declaration-package or qualified
    CODE-slot lookup, run once per source occurrence during construction, and
    may return a regex, predicate, or check object. Every unescaped leading `&`
    is reserved for this syntax; constraints remain synchronous validation and
    never coerce values.
  - Declarative HTTP, WebSocket, and SSE leaves now share the explicit
    `constraints` option. Composed reverse routing preserves the exact
    normalized predicates from leaf and mount source Patterns, so `path_for`
    and `url_for` neither re-resolve providers nor apply explicit constraints
    twice.
  - The large modular application example now requires Perl 5.40, uses
    signatures, and demonstrates `Types::Standard::Int` providers. Type::Tiny
    is a test/example prerequisite only, not a PAGI::Tools runtime dependency.
  - Every PAGI::Routing Mount has exactly one named target: `app` or `routes`.
    `app` retains a native coderef or instantiated component; immutable Router
    apps remain inspectable, while other apps are opaque. `routes` constructs a
    complete child Router. Named and unnamed Mounts share the same model;
    `Mount->router`, `target`, and `is_raw` are removed. Exact prefixes and
    their trailing-slash spelling both reach child `/` without redirecting.
  - All immutable declarative middleware lists now accept the additive
    bare-factory shorthand; the chat showcase uses it at its Compose application root.
  - New PAGI::Compose is an optional immutable application-root description
    combining either declarative routes or one existing PAGI component with
    application-wide pure event middleware and explicit startup/shutdown
    callbacks. It owns lifespan without delegating it, requires server-provided
    state when callbacks are configured, normalizes immediate/Future callback
    completion, and adds an outer idempotent application-root HEAD boundary.
    A directly compiled Router keeps its own HEAD boundary; the shared marker
    leaves the outermost participant as wire owner so all middleware sees the
    complete representation before suppression.
  - New PAGI::Routing provides the immutable, declaration-ordered route tree
    shared by all three public frontends. Normal HTTP handlers receive one
    PAGI::Request and return application values; native CODE Routes use
    `as_app` and retain native channel ownership. The release includes inline and application mounts,
    route/mount/router pure event middleware, direct PAGI::WebSocket and PAGI::SSE
    handlers, automatic HEAD wire suppression, Router-owned HTTP outcomes,
    first-seen method evidence, synchronous constraints, reverse URLs,
    request-local matched-route frames, inspection, and an executable example.
    PAGI::App::Router and PAGI::Endpoint::Router materialize the same model.
  - Application positions accept coderefs or instantiated `to_app` objects and
    reject package strings. This includes Route endpoints, `app`,
    `http_default`, Test
    Client `app`, Lifespan `app`/`wrap`, Middleware Builder mount/fallback,
    URLMap, and Cascade targets. Middleware class strings remain accepted
    because middleware descriptors define their loading/construction/wrap
    contract. PAGI follows Starlette's Route/Mount/Router/application topology
    rather than copying every Starlette Request method. It deliberately differs
    through explicit imports for optional capabilities, direct protocol versus
    native positions, validation without coercion, slash logical names and
    relative lookup, first-class SSE,
    HTTP-only `http_default`, pure PAGI middleware, and Compose-owned root
    lifespan. Starlette's multiprotocol Router `default` was considered and not
    copied. OpenAPI/schema generation remains deferred; no `schema` or
    `include_in_schema` placeholder is shipped.
  - Declarative mount prefixes accept both the exact prefix and its slash form
    without redirecting, deliberately differing from Starlette's default
    slash behavior. ReverseProxy and TrustedHosts remain HTTP-only; routing
    does not ship the separately planned cross-protocol compatibility changes.
  - BREAKING: normal Router handlers now receive the selected protocol object
    directly: PAGI::Request for HTTP, PAGI::WebSocket for WebSocket, and
    PAGI::SSE for SSE. PAGI::Request construction is strict HTTP-only
    `($scope, $receive)`. Native applications and all middleware retain the
    `($scope, $receive, $send)` contract. HTTP handlers return application
    values directly or through a Future.
  - New PAGI::Routing::URL, PAGI::State, PAGI::CSRF, and PAGI::Transport
    facades make Router-, lifespan-, middleware-, and server-owned capabilities
    explicit. PAGI::Stash and PAGI::Session now accept the same Request or raw
    scope sources. Only `app_state` is exported for State because `state(...)`
    conflicts with Perl's declarator; its temporary hash-dereference bridge
    warns unless PAGI_SILENCE_STATE_HASHREF_WARNING is exactly 1.
  - PAGI::Request retains intrinsic HTTP input, strict path parameters, State,
    and minimal connection access. `is_disconnected` is now tri-state and
    returns undef when no connection capability exists; advanced flow control
    uses PAGI::Transport. Router URL, Stash, Session, CSRF, and the removed
    outgoing response shortcuts do not transfer to Request, and the former
    `PAGI::Request->response` bridge is removed.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::Router replaces `new_context` with the explicit HTTP-only
    `new_request($scope, $receive)` helper; compiled dispatch constructs direct
    protocol objects independently of that override seam.
  - New PAGI::Authority centralizes strict validation and scope-derived authority
    formatting. The HTTP Request authority accessor now returns only a
    single structurally valid Host field (including an explicit port), returns
    undef when it is absent, and reject malformed or duplicate fields. This is
    intentional security hardening; raw, last-value header('host')
    remains the compatibility escape hatch.
  - PAGI::Middleware::TrustedHosts and ::HTTPSRedirect now fail closed with a
    generic HTTP 400 for malformed or duplicate Host data. HTTPSRedirect uses
    the validated server tuple only when Host is absent, rather than inventing
    a fallback authority.
  - PAGI::Middleware::ReverseProxy accepts a trusted X-Forwarded-Host exactly
    once, validates it as one authority, replaces every existing Host pair with
    one rewritten pair, and invalidates the request-header cache. Its proxy
    trust and HTTP-only gates are unchanged; this release does not extend these
    middleware behaviors to other protocols.
  - New Features: new internal PAGI::SendValidation validates send-sequencing
    for HTTP, WebSocket, SSE, and lifespan scopes (unknown/missing event
    types, illegal ordering, undeclared extensions), mirroring the reference
    server's PAGI::Server::EventValidator categories and intent. This is
    toolkit-internal infrastructure (not a public application-facing API) --
    it exists so PAGI::Test::Client's strict mode and the development Lint
    middleware share one validation core instead of each reimplementing it.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::Client's HTTP C<$send> is now strict, with no
    lenient mode: illegal events (unrecognized or missing type, out-of-order
    events such as a duplicate http.response.start or a body before it,
    undeclared http.response.trailers -- the malformed/unknown_type/sequence
    categories) now fail the returned Future instead of being silently
    absorbed. An app that returns without reaching a legal terminal state
    (no terminal body chunk, or declared trailers never sent -- incomplete)
    is now reported as an abnormal server_error disconnect with a warning,
    not a clean completion; it previously hard-died with a generic
    "forgot to await" message instead. An app that returns without ever
    sending http.response.start now yields the server's synthesized 500
    backstop (was a phantom empty 200). Every scope now advertises
    pagi.version 0.4 / spec_version 0.3 (was 0.2), matching the released
    server.
  - BREAKING: direction change: when an app throws B<after> its HTTP
    response already reached a legal terminal state, PAGI::Test::Client now
    returns the real, already-complete response (firing on_complete, not
    on_disconnect) instead of overwriting it with a synthetic 500 --
    mirroring the server, since the exception did not corrupt anything
    already sent to the wire. A stray warning documents the post-completion
    exception. An exception before the response is complete keeps the
    existing 500 + server_error behavior.
  - PAGI::SendValidation's websocket rules gained the
    C<websocket.http.response> denial extension (C<websocket.http.response.start>
    / C<.body>), mirroring the reference server's C<denial>/C<denial_complete>
    states: recognized as a legal denial before C<websocket.accept>, illegal
    everywhere else. A completed extension denial reports C<complete> true but
    C<closed> false -- it is not a C<websocket.close> -- matching how SSE's
    C<decline_complete> state already behaves.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::WebSocket's C<$send> is now strict, on the same
    terms as the HTTP path: illegal events (per the websocket rules above,
    plus the pre-existing accept/send/close ordering) fail the returned
    Future instead of being silently accepted.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::WebSocket's synthesized C<websocket.disconnect>
    is now delivered exactly once, carrying a truthful C<code> and
    C<reason> (previously repeated on every subsequent C<receive> call, and
    dropped the reason). An app that keeps calling C<receive> after
    disconnect now hangs (the correct failure mode -- a real transport has
    gone silent) rather than getting a phantom disconnect on every call.
    New C<< $ws->simulate_abnormal_close(code => ..., reason => ...) >>
    injects an abnormal close (default code 1006) the same way, for testing
    a specific abnormal-close reason token.
  - BREAKING: An app C<websocket.send> after the app's own
    C<websocket.close> now fails the Future (was silently appended to the
    client's readable stream). A C<websocket.send> after the test (peer)
    side closed is now a tolerated no-op instead -- dropped, not delivered,
    but does not fail the app's Future -- mirroring how a real server
    tolerates a write racing a peer that already went away.
  - BREAKING: An app that sends C<websocket.close> before
    C<websocket.accept> (a portable denial) no longer croaks
    "WebSocket connection not accepted"; L<PAGI::Test::WebSocket> reports
    the closed/denied state instead, and a completed
    C<websocket.http.response.*> extension denial is now recognized the
    same way.
  - PAGI::Test::SSE's C<$send> is likewise now strict via
    L<PAGI::SendValidation>'s sse rules, and now recognizes an
    app-initiated C<sse.close> (previously ignored: the app could send it
    and nothing happened).
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::SSE's decline (C<sse.http.response.start> /
    C<.body>) is now recognized, mirroring the server:
    L<PAGI::Test::Client>'s C<sse> method returns a L<PAGI::Test::Response>
    with the declined status/body instead of croaking "SSE connection not
    started". No C<sse.disconnect> is delivered for a decline -- the stream
    never started.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::SSE's synthesized C<sse.disconnect> is now
    delivered exactly once, carrying an explicit C<reason> (default
    C<client_closed>) instead of being repeated reason-less on every
    subsequent C<receive> call. C<< $sse->close >> now takes an optional
    C<$reason> argument.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::Client's HTTP responses are now H1-flavored:
    app-supplied C<Transfer-Encoding>/C<Connection> headers are stripped
    (warned once per occurrence), mirroring PAGI::Server::Connection's HTTP/1.1
    rule -- C<Upgrade>/C<TE>/C<Keep-Alive> are untouched (deliberately narrower
    than the server's HTTP/2 six-name strip). A C<Date> header is added when
    the app didn't supply one; an app-supplied C<Date> is preserved verbatim.
  - BREAKING: Every PAGI::Test::Client scope now advertises an C<extensions>
    key: C<{}> for C<http> and C<sse> (this mock has no transport to flush, so
    C<http.fullflush> stays genuinely unadvertised and unreachable through it),
    and C<{ 'websocket.http.response' => {} }> for C<websocket> -- the SAME
    shared hashref now threaded into L<PAGI::SendValidation> too, replacing
    PAGI::Test::WebSocket's previously independently-hardcoded advertisement
    of that extension (single source of truth; see
    L<PAGI::Test::Client/SCOPE EXTENSIONS>).
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::Client's lifespan C<$send> is now strict too: a
    result reported for the wrong phase (for example C<lifespan.shutdown.complete>
    sent during the startup phase) fails the app's C<await $send-E<gt>(...)>
    instead of being silently accepted. C<stop> now waits for and C<die>s on
    C<lifespan.shutdown.failed>, symmetric with how C<start> already handled
    C<lifespan.startup.failed> -- a failing shutdown hook previously failed
    silently.
  - BREAKING: PAGI::Test::ConnectionState's C<disconnect_future> is now
    modeled fully (was always C<undef>): pending before any end, resolves with
    the reason on an abnormal disconnect, and -- the sharpest break from a
    naive always-a-Future implementation -- stays pending forever if first
    requested B<after> a clean completion (use C<on_complete> for that case).
    New C<response_complete> method: always defined (this mock always tracks
    completion -- per L<PAGI::Spec::Www>, C<undef> signals the fixed
    capability "server does not track completion", not a per-request
    state), C<0> before the response reaches its terminal state, C<1> after.
  - BREAKING: Body readers no longer treat a mid-body C<http.disconnect> as
    a clean end of body. C<< PAGI::Request->body >>/C<text>/C<json>/
    C<form_params> now croak with C<"Request body incomplete: client
    disconnected mid-body ($reason)"> instead of silently handing back
    whatever bytes had arrived as if the body were complete; a second call
    fails identically without re-awaiting the connection. The same fix
    applies to L<PAGI::Middleware>'s C<buffer_request_body>,
    L<PAGI::Middleware::FormBody>, L<PAGI::Middleware::JSONBody>, and
    L<PAGI::Request::MultiPartHandler>. An immediate disconnect before any
    body bytes ever arrive is still treated as a legitimate empty body, not
    a truncation. L<PAGI::Request::BodyStream> gets a new C<truncated>
    accessor for the streaming API (C<next_chunk> still returns C<undef> on
    disconnect -- streaming consumers may have legitimate uses for partial
    data -- so C<truncated> plus the optional
    C<< $req->connection->disconnect_reason >> is how you tell that apart
    from a real end of body).
  - PAGI::Response::Writer's C<close> no longer skips C<on_close>
    cleanup when the terminal send fails: callbacks now fire (and the
    callback array is cleared) whether the send succeeds or fails, and the
    C<Future> C<close> returns fails with the original send error instead of
    swallowing it. A second C<close()> call remains a safe no-op either way.
  - PAGI::Response::Stream's private application-delivery path no longer leaves
    the writer open
    and its C<on_close> callbacks unrun when the stream callback throws.
    Per L<PAGI::Spec::Www/Application Left a Response Incomplete>, sending
    the terminal event on that path would falsely certify a truncated
    response as complete, so private Stream delivery still sends B<no> terminal
    event and still re-raises the exception (the wire abort is deliberate, and the
    server observes it as C<on_disconnect> with reason C<server_error>) --
    but the writer is now marked closed and its C<on_close> callbacks run
    for local cleanup before the exception propagates.
  - PAGI::App::Proxy (demo-only) now dehops both directions and speaks
    HTTP/1.0 with an explicit C<Connection: close> to the backend: client
    request headers are dehopped via L<PAGI::Headers/dehop> before
    forwarding, the backend request line is downgraded from HTTP/1.1 to
    HTTP/1.0, and the backend response headers are dehopped before
    C<http.response.start>. HTTP/1.0+close rules out a chunked backend
    response (chunked is HTTP/1.1-only) and any backend keep-alive, so the
    existing read-to-EOF is now provably the correct response framing
    (previously a backend that kept the connection open per HTTP/1.1
    defaults could hang the proxy's read forever).
  - PAGI::Lifespan now tracks a pending/started/shutdown phase and ignores
    an out-of-phase lifespan.startup or lifespan.shutdown message (with one
    warning) instead of re-running startup handlers or attempting shutdown
    before startup -- hardening against a misbehaving server or client,
    mirroring PAGI::Compose::Compiler::_run_lifespan's existing guard.
  - PAGI::Compose::ResponseGuard: a response that declares C<http.response.start>
    C<trailers =E<gt> 1> but completes after its terminal body chunk without
    ever sending C<http.response.trailers> is now a typed incomplete-response
    failure (new C<awaiting_trailers> stage on
    PAGI::Exception::IncompleteResponse) instead of silently passing the
    guard. Also, rejecting a response body sent before response start is now
    a failed C<Future> handed back from the C<send> call, not a synchronous
    C<die> -- contract parity with how the real server rejects an invalid
    send; the typed exception and reject-without-forwarding behavior are
    unchanged.

0.002002 - 2026-07-15
  - PAGI::Middleware::Session: non-'http' scopes that carry a headers key
    (WebSocket, SSE upgrades) now get a READ-ONLY session populated the same
    way 'http' does, instead of being skipped; nothing is ever saved for them,
    and an unresolvable id yields an empty hashref rather than a missing key.
    See the new "NON-HTTP SCOPES" POD section.
  - PAGI::App::Router: an unmatched WebSocket route now uses the optional
    websocket.http.response denial extension only when advertised; otherwise
    it closes before acceptance so the server supplies the bare 403 response.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: new 'enforce' config -- 'header' (default,
    unchanged: unsafe methods need a matching header token or a 403) or 'app'
    (issue-only: mint/stash the token for every method, never auto-reject, and
    let the app validate). 'app' exists because the middleware cannot read
    form params without buffering the body stream.
  - The former Context facade gained csrf_token and csrf_verify (timing-safe
    token checks). Both inherited into PAGI::Nano contexts.
  - REMOVED PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's 'token_param' config: it was dead (the
    form-field lookup was never implemented). Use enforce => 'app' with the
    facade's csrf_verify method for form tokens.
  - PAGI::Utils::SecureCompare: new module for the constant-time string compare
    shared by PAGI::Middleware::CSRF and the former facade.
  - Docs: PAGI::Middleware::CSRF's USAGE section no longer contradicts itself
    (a JS getCookie() against an HttpOnly cookie); it now documents the header
    and form flows separately.
  - t/integration/sse-decline-end-to-end.t now skips unless PAGI::Server is
    >= 0.002005, the release that added the sse.http.response.* decline
    protocol it exercises. Fixes a CPAN Testers FAIL against 0.002001 (older
    servers answer an unmatched SSE route with 500 instead of 404).
  - PAGI::Middleware::Auth::Bearer: _secure_compare now rejects undef arguments
    instead of numifying them, so two undef (or undef vs empty) signatures no
    longer compare equal.
  - PAGI::Middleware::CSRF: new 'secure' config (default 0) to set the Secure
    attribute on the CSRF cookie; enable for HTTPS deployments.
  - PAGI::WebSocket / PAGI::SSE: a per-message callback that dies in the
    iteration helpers (each_message/each_text/each_bytes/each_json, SSE each()
    and every()) now runs on_close cleanup before re-raising, instead of
    bypassing it. WebSocket::run() is unchanged -- it isolates errors and keeps
    serving.
  - The former protocol-specific Context wrappers now fire the underlying
    object's on_close when a terminal disconnect is driven through their run
    method (previously it only recorded the reason, so on_close never fired and
    is_closed/is_connected lied). Both also gained on_close() stubs pointing at
    $c->websocket->on_close / $c->sse->on_close.
  - PAGI::Test::Client: start() now drives the event loop during lifespan
    startup and dies on failure, instead of busy-waiting a 5s deadline without
    advancing the loop (which hung off-loop startup hooks and ignored
    lifespan.startup.failed).

0.002001 - 2026-06-30
  - PAGI::Endpoint / PAGI::Endpoint::Router: route-level event middleware is now
    spec-pure factory form, composed at build time. The vestigial call() and the
    old 4-arg ($scope, $receive, $send, $next) middleware form are removed; the
    endpoint owns the value-flow chain and responds at the framework boundary
    (await $next->() returns the handler's response value). WS/SSE route-level
    middleware now fail loudly (unsupported), and a value-flow non-response error
    names the offending method.
  - PAGI::SSE: close() now sends an explicit sse.close event (with an optional
    server-side reason) and wakes a parked run(); close() is async so on_close
    callbacks run to completion.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::SSE: await keepalive() so the sse.keepalive event is actually
    emitted on a fully-async server.
  - The endpoint and router dispatch paths formerly shared one nominal
    Response predicate so their diagnostics stayed consistent. The current
    application-valued contract supersedes that older release behavior.
  - PAGI::SSE and its former facade gained is_connected, derived from the SSE
    wrapper's own state machine (true while started and not closed), mirroring
    PAGI::WebSocket. The facade overrode the base method, which
    reads pagi.connection (not applicable to sse) and therefore wrongly reported
    a live stream as disconnected. Like the WebSocket counterpart it is "not
    falsely stale" rather than a live network probe.
  - PAGI::WebSocket->deny() no longer records the HTTP denial status as a
    WebSocket close code; close_code is undef after an HTTP denial (a denial
    sends a response, not a close frame). The bare-403 fallback still reports
    1008.
  - PAGI::WebSocket try_send_text/bytes/json no longer fabricate a 1006
    "Connection lost" close (and no longer mark the socket closed) when a send
    errors. A failed send returns false without mutating connection state; a
    real disconnect is still detected via the websocket.disconnect event.
  - Docs: PAGI::SSE scope caching described accurately (cached while
    referenced via a weak ref, not a guaranteed singleton);
    PAGI::Request::Negotiate content-negotiation example reads the Accept
    header through PAGI::Request instead of the (fatal) $scope->{headers}{...};
    try_send_* documented as best-effort with a send-method ladder; a cookbook
    recipe on declining an SSE request (404 / 401 / 204 stop-reconnect);
    examples and Endpoint POD migrated off the removed 4-arg middleware form.
  - REMOVED PAGI::Middleware::WebSocket::Compression. permessage-deflate
    (RFC 7692) is a WebSocket framing-layer feature -- a compressed message is
    signalled by the RSV1 frame bit, which only the server's framing layer can
    set. A middleware at the PAGI event layer can deflate the payload bytes but
    cannot flag the frame, so the deflated bytes went out in a normal RSV1=0
    frame (garbage on the wire). It also wrote a non-spec `extensions` key on
    websocket.accept (the event allows only `subprotocol`/`headers`), which the
    reference server ignores -- and that server explicitly does not implement
    permessage-deflate (RSV bits forced to 0). If WebSocket compression is
    wanted, it must be a server feature, not an application middleware.
  - PAGI::App::Router: an unmatched SSE or WebSocket route now returns a real
    404 instead of crashing the connection. The 404 is emitted with the event
    family that matches the scope -- sse.http.response.* on SSE and
    websocket.http.response.* on WebSocket, plain http.response.* on HTTP --
    since those scopes reject a plain http.response.* before a stream or
    handshake starts. A custom not_found app still takes over for every scope
    type. Requires a server supporting the sse.http.response.* decline events
    (PAGI-Server 0.002005+).

0.002000 - 2026-06-26
  - Split out of the PAGI distribution into its own distribution (git history
    preserved).
  - New PAGI::Tools module anchors the distribution.
  - The application runner ships in PAGI-Server as PAGI::Server::Runner.
  - B<BREAKING>: PAGI::Response is now a value — factories build detached
    responses, body methods return $self, respond($send) sends, to_app makes any
    response mountable; Endpoint HTTP handlers RETURN a response value
    (await $res->json(...) no longer sends).
  - B<BREAKING>: PAGI::Request->configure/->config removed; path_param strict and
    multipart limits are now per-call options.
  - PAGI::Headers: ordered, case-insensitive, multi-value header container
    (rejects undef values; to_hash); Response and Request route headers through it.
  - is_sent now reads response_started off the pagi.connection object, not a scope
    scalar, so it survives middleware's scope copy. Adds PAGI::Test::ConnectionState.
  - PAGI::Response->has_body_source: predicate for whether a body source was
    registered.
  - PAGI::Response: charset follows the body — text/html/send gain
    "; charset=utf-8", json() stays bare application/json.
  - PAGI::Utils::to_app coerces coderefs, component objects, and class names; every
    composition point accepts anything it accepts.
  - builder's enable() accepts configured middleware instances.
  - PAGI::App::Router: coderef route middleware can transform the channel.
  - PAGI::Endpoint::Router route middleware are value-flow (await $next->() returns
    the handler's response); App::Router dispatch returns the matched route's value.
  - The former Context facade gained text/html/json/redirect shorthands,
    assert_http/websocket/sse guards, on_default catch-all, raw_send accessor.
  - Backpressure/lifecycle helpers: on_complete, buffered_amount/high_water_mark/
    low_water_mark, on_high_water/on_drain/is_writable, WebSocket->deny().
  - PAGI::Request->multipart_stream: pull-based streaming multipart parsing with
    app-controlled sinks; mutually exclusive with the buffered body methods.
  - The standalone Redirect and NotFound applications build a PAGI::Response
    value (modules kept for the dynamic case).
  - PAGI::Lifespan coerces its app arg via to_app, and reports shutdown-handler
    failures instead of swallowing them.
  - Spec: URLMap sets root_path; WrapCGI builds SCRIPT_NAME from root_path.
  - Canonical scope-adding middleware route through the modify_scope helper.
  - Documented the PAGI::Response subclassing seam for framework authors.
  - Docs: recipes are now PAGI::Tools::Cookbook; Tutorial/Cookbook examples
    corrected against the current API; chat-showcase example uses PAGI::App::Router
    + PAGI::Lifespan.
  - For changes prior to 0.002000, see the Changes file of the PAGI distribution
    (versions up to 0.001023).
